# Overview
#
# A hedge is a word or phrase that keeps the writer from committing to a
# claim (`a relatively small number of objects`, `the test should
# pass`). The hedge softens the claim without adding information, so the
# reader cannot tell how far the claim holds. The fix states the claim
# or drops the hedge. A writer with real doubt can name the condition
# that the claim depends on, so the message offers that fix beside
# stating the claim. The rule sits at Optional, because each word here
# sometimes carries meaning, and `should` before a result also states a
# requirement.
#
# Expression
#
# `main` lists `relatively`, `virtually`, and `practically`, and one
# branch for `should`. That branch counts `should` after a noun and
# before a verb in `result`, which lists the verbs that name a visible
# result (`the test should pass`, `the output should look like this`).
# There `should` hedges the result of a step, so the fix states it (`the
# test passes`). Weir has no lookbehind, so the filter consumes the word
# before `should` and narrows the finding back to `should`.
#
# Harper's part-of-speech tagger marks each word with a tag for its word
# class. The word before `should` must carry `NOUN`, the tag for a noun,
# and must not carry `PRON`, the tag for a pronoun. It must also stay
# out of `person`, which lists the words for a person, such as `caller`.
#
# False Positives
#
# `relatively` sometimes states a real comparison (`relatively few nodes
# run the agent, compared with the fleet`). Most hits do not give a
# baseline (`a relatively small number of objects`), so the fix gives
# the number or the baseline.
#
# `virtually` and `practically` each read two ways. `virtually` means
# `almost` or names a virtual copy (`virtually hoisted`), and
# `practically` means `almost` or `in practice`. The plain sense is
# sometimes the one meant.
#
# `effectively` stays out. In 29 of its 39 hits in the measure.py corpus
# it meant `in effect` (`higher values are effectively ignored`), where
# dropping it makes the claim false, and in the other 10 it named a
# manner (`use labels effectively`).
#
# After a person such as `caller`, `should` gives advice (`the caller
# should pass a listener`), so the `should` branch skips the words in
# `person`. `return`, `contain`, and `include` stay out of `result`,
# because API docs state a requirement with them (`implementations
# should return a copy`).
#
# Related Rules
#
# The Optional ProseHedge holds `relatively`, `virtually`,
# `practically`, and `should` before a visible result, because each
# sometimes carries meaning. The Recommended ProseHedge holds every
# other hedge. It reports `it should work`, so the `should` branch here
# skips a pronoun.
#
# `potentially` stays out, because ProseDoubleHedge reports `could
# potentially`.
#
# Measurement
#
# The rule hit 46 lines in the measure.py corpus, and 18 of 20 sampled
# hits softened a claim or a result. The misfires stated a requirement
# (`all tests should pass regardless`) or a design (`what the pool
# should look like`).
#
# The `should` branch hit 19 lines in the measure.py corpus, and 15 of
# 19 hedged a result. The misfires stated a requirement, a condition, or
# a design (`if any of those instances should fail`), so the branch is a
# suggestion.
expr person [caller, callers, consumer, consumers, user, users, reader, readers, developer, developers, operator, operators, administrator, administrators]
expr result [pass, fail, print, display, show, look, appear, succeed, output, produce, compile]
expr main [relatively, virtually, practically, <(<NOUN, !PRON, !@person> should @result), should>]
let message "Hedge. State the claim, name the condition it depends on, or drop it."
let description "Flags `relatively`, `virtually`, and `practically`, which soften a claim or read two ways, and `should` before a visible result."
let becomes ""
let kind "Style"
allows "Few nodes run the agent."
allows "The flag disables the cache."
allows "Higher values are effectively ignored."
allows "The test passes."
allows "The caller should pass a listener."
allows "Implementations should return a copy."
